# {{alternative case form of|Eid}}

===Etymology 2===
From English dialectal {{m|en|eid}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|eið||an isthmus, neck of land}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*aidiją||isthmus, strait}}, of uncertain origin, but probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁y-||to go}}. Cognate with {{etyl|is|-}} {{m|is|eið}}, {{m|is|eiði}}, {{etyl|fo|-}} {{m|fo|eið}}, {{m|fo|eiði||isthmus}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{m|no|eid||isthmus}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|ed}}. Compare {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|eō||go, proceed|pos=verb}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ed}}, {{l|en|aith}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland|lang=en}} An [[isthmus]] or narrow neck of land jutting out into the sea; a [[sandbank]] cast up by the sea across the head of an open [[bight]] or inlet and having a lagoon inside it.

